course: reflection
lesson: 29
title: Getting help: when sadness or worry won't go away
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most sad or worried feelings come and go, but some stay and stay: for two months Alfie felt flat, couldn't be bothered with football, was tired all the time and cried in the shower for no reason, and he kept thinking: "I've got nothing to be sad about. My life's fine. What's wrong with me? I can't tell anyone, they'll think I'm being dramatic."
explain: It started small after his gran moved away; week by week it got greyer; he said "fine" a hundred times; when he finally told his mum he cried for an hour, then slept better than he had for weeks; with the family doctor and a school counsellor, it lifted slowly over months, and he started football again bit by bit.
explain: What he came to understand: sadness or worry that goes on for weeks is a sign to get help, not something to hide; you don't need a "big reason"; asking for help is strong, not dramatic, and doctors and counsellors help children with this every day.
explain: Help has many doors: a parent, a teacher, the school nurse, a counsellor, a doctor, or a children's helpline, where real, trained people listen; a note to a teacher works if saying it out loud is too hard.
explain: Some things must never be kept secret: if a friend says they wish they weren't here, or you find out a friend is hurting themselves, tell a trusted adult straight away, even if they asked you not to; you can also call a helpline.
fact: Sadness or worry that lasts for weeks and gets in the way of normal life is a sign to tell a trusted adult and get help.
fact: You don't need a "big reason" to feel low; feelings can grow slowly, and asking for help is strong, not dramatic.
fact: Depression is an illness where low mood stays for a long time; it can be treated, and doctors and counsellors help with it.
fact: Anxiety is a lot of worry that won't go away; it can cause headaches, tummy aches and trouble sleeping, many children have it, and it can be helped.
fact: A counsellor is someone trained to help with feelings; going to one is like going to a doctor for your feelings, and it is normal and smart.
fact: If a friend says they wish they weren't here, or you learn a friend is hurting themselves, tell a trusted adult straight away, even if they asked you not to; you can also call a helpline.
fact: A helpline is a phone number where real, trained people listen and help; in the UK, Childline is a free helpline for children, and many other countries have their own.
fact: Confidential means kept private; helplines and counsellors keep what you say private unless someone is in danger.
fact: A good teacher who believes a child about bullying acts quickly; writing a note is a fine way to tell if speaking feels too hard.
fact: When a teacher asks you to stay behind and asks "are you okay?", it usually means they noticed you are struggling, not that you are in trouble.
fact: Therapy means talking with someone trained to help with feelings, such as anger or sadness; going shows someone wants to get better, not that they are "crazy".
fact: Trauma is the lasting effect of something very frightening; flashbacks are sudden, vivid memories of it; there is no time limit for getting over it, and counsellors can help.
fact: Unkind words about people who get help come from not understanding; there is no shame in it.
exercise: Alfie has felt flat for two months with "nothing to be sad about". What is true? | Low mood lasting weeks is a sign to get help | He is being dramatic and should just cheer up | It will definitely go away if he keeps it hidden | You need a big reason before you're allowed to feel sad
exercise: How did things change after Alfie told his mum? | He cried, slept better, and with help it lifted over months | It got much worse, because saying it out loud made it more real | It all disappeared the very next morning | Nothing changed, so telling was pointless
exercise: What did NOT help Alfie? | Saying "I'm fine" for two months | Telling his mum how he felt | Seeing the family doctor about it | A school counsellor once a week
exercise: Mia worries about tests, her parents and germs, and gets headaches and can't sleep. What might this be? | Anxiety, which lots of children have and can be helped | Just being a big baby about normal things that everyone deals with | A cold that will pass in a day | Proof that something bad will happen
exercise: What helped Mia? | The counsellor, breathing exercises and a worry box | Keeping all her worries secret so nobody would think she was weird | Reading about germs every night | Staying home from school for good
exercise: Josh's friend tells him, "Sometimes I wish I wasn't here any more." What should Josh do? | Tell a trusted adult straight away, even if his friend is angry | Keep it secret forever, because his friend trusted him and asked him to | Wait a week to see if his friend says it again | Try to sort it out on his own without telling any grown-up at all
exercise: Ruby is scared her teacher won't believe her about bullying. What helped? | Writing it in a note to the teacher | Waiting for the bullying to stop by itself | Telling only her best friend in secret | Bullying the bullies back at playtime
exercise: Kids say an unkind word about anyone who sees the school counsellor. Kofi needs help. What is true? | Seeing a counsellor is normal, with no shame in it | Only people who are really ill go to counsellors | He should wait until he's an adult to get help | The kids are right, so he should keep quiet
exercise: Grace calls a children's helpline and wonders, "Is it a robot?" What is true? | Real, trained people answer and listen | It's just a recorded message with no person | It tells your mum everything straight away | It's only for grown-ups, not for children
exercise: What does "confidential" mean for a helpline? | Kept private, unless someone is in danger | Told to the whole school and both parents by the next day | Recorded and put on the internet | Kept secret even if someone is in danger
exercise: Leo's teacher asks him to stay after class. He thinks he's in trouble. What was it really? | The teacher noticed he wasn't himself and wanted to help | The teacher wanted to give him extra homework for being slow in class | The teacher was angry about his handwriting | The teacher wanted him to tidy the classroom
exercise: Ellie's dad goes to therapy for his anger. She wonders, "Is he crazy?" What is true? | Getting help shows he wants to get better | Only people who are really crazy ever go to therapy | Therapy is a punishment for being angry | It means Dad will be angry forever
exercise: Archie is scared all the time months after something frightening. He thinks he should be over it. What is true? | There's no time limit, and counsellors can help | Everyone gets over everything in a month | He is weak for still being scared | It will go away by itself if he just never thinks about it again
exercise: Holly finds out her friend has been hurting herself, and her friend asks her not to tell. What should Holly do? | Tell a trusted adult straight away, even though she was asked not to | Keep the promise, because a friend asked her to and friends come first always | Tell the other kids so that they can all keep a close eye on her friend instead | Wait until her friend feels like telling someone
exercise: What can Josh or Holly do as well as telling a trusted adult? | Call a helpline | Post about it online | Keep a secret diary | Wait and watch what happens
test: Alfie's mate goes, "you're just being dramatic, man, everyone's tired." ("Dramatic" means making a big fuss.) Two months of feeling flat — what's the truth? | Feeling low for weeks is a real sign to tell an adult | His mate is right; it's just ordinary tiredness and fuss, it'll pass | He should hide it so nobody thinks he's weak | Boys don't get low moods, so it can't be real
test: What would probably happen if Mia kept her worries secret for another term? | The worry and headaches would likely keep growing | The worries would all vanish on their own by the end of the week | She would become the bravest girl in class | The germs would all disappear
test: Josh's friend says, "Promise you won't tell anyone, yeah?" What's the rule? | Some things must be told to a trusted adult straight away, promise or not | A promise must always be kept, whatever it is about and whoever made it | He should only tell anyone if his friend says it again, maybe twice | He should tell the other kids, not grown-ups
test: Ruby's mum from Belfast says, "Wee pet, write it down for your teacher if you can't say it." ("Wee" means little; "pet" means dear.) Why is that good advice? | A note is a fine way to tell when speaking is too hard | Teachers only read notes and never really listen to what kids say | It means Ruby will never have to talk again | Notes make the bullying stop by magic
test: Kofi hears, "Counselling's for weirdos, bro." What's true? | It's like going to a doctor for feelings | Counsellors only see kids who are in trouble | He should feel ashamed for needing any help | Strong people never need any help from anyone
test: Grace's mate says, "Helplines are just robots reading scripts, deffo." ("Deffo" means definitely.) What's the truth? | Helplines have real, trained people who listen | Helplines are only answering machines | Helplines tell your parents straight away | Helplines are only for adults with big problems, not for children
test: Leo's mate goes, "staying behind? you're cooked, bro." ("Cooked" means in big trouble.) What was really happening? | His teacher noticed he wasn't okay and wanted to help | He was in massive trouble for something | The teacher was going to call the police about something he'd done | He had forgotten his homework again
test: In a film, a character goes to therapy and everyone calls him crazy. What does Ellie learn from her real dad? | Therapy helped him get calmer, and getting help is brave | Therapy makes people crazier than they were before they started | Therapy is only for people in films | Her dad should have kept it a secret
test: Archie's uncle says, "It's been months, lad, get over it." What does Archie need to hear instead? | There's no time limit, and a counsellor can help | His uncle is right; he should be well over it after all these months | He should stop thinking and it'll go | He's weak for not getting over it faster
test: Holly's friend begs, "Don't tell. Please. You'll make it worse." What is the right thing for Holly to do? | Tell a trusted adult straight away, even though she was asked not to | Keep it secret so that her friend keeps trusting her and stays her friend | Tell only her other friends and nobody else | Wait for a better time to tell someone, maybe next term when things are calmer
test: A girl from Sydney asks, "Can I ring a helpline if I'm not in Britain?" What's true? | Many countries have free helplines for children | Only British children are allowed helplines | Helplines only work if you live in English or British cities | Children can only call with a parent there
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises low mood persisting for a period of weeks? | It constitutes a sign that assistance ought to be sought from a trusted adult | It constitutes evidence of a dramatic disposition requiring concealment | It invariably resolves within twenty-four hours without intervention | It is experienced solely by persons with a substantial reason for sadness
exam: It is given that a friend has expressed a wish not to be here, and has requested secrecy. Identify the correct course of action. | A trusted adult shall be informed forthwith notwithstanding the request; a helpline may also be contacted | The request for secrecy shall be honoured in all circumstances | The matter shall be deferred until the friend repeats the statement | The matter shall be disclosed solely to other pupils
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning children's helplines. | They are staffed by trained persons and treat calls as confidential unless a person is in danger | They are automated services incapable of conversation | They are obliged to inform the caller's parents of each and every call received | They are available exclusively to persons over eighteen years of age
exam: In respect of attendance at counselling or therapy, determine which statement is correct. | It is a normal and sensible means of obtaining help with one's feelings | It indicates that the attendee is of unsound mind | It is a disciplinary measure imposed by the school for misconduct | It is available only to adults in employment
